#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0626020c-ffd0-5d52-be60-af389fc83a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278648} {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} {:answer/id #uuid "84467733-f429-5feb-8388-07e2d51f176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279778} {:answer/id #uuid "af416bf2-0b93-5782-b6de-1f1c2d402fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uvw)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280143} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f31297-6e1d-54c7-be43-6fdd25ab29fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(uvw)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbffbbf-30e0-555b-b5e7-55199b3bfffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82aa7a5f-aede-5ec9-8984-8cdea4eb6f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-161807" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}|+2yz|\\begin{aligned}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "408e29c9-6522-5467-9ab8-def9fe8cfb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324490} {:answer/id #uuid "69224e59-e1c5-54cd-8748-b2d597b5cd76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{w}{u}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324848} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1e869c-a3b7-584b-8e0f-e99423947eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325004} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5ec600-18ee-54e3-bb28-7c22c6783223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} :answer-field/id #uuid "65802821-8118-5ece-8435-a69816ad88f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f85af4c0-4520-5614-952b-cb9d6d06c6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39300" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =(\\frac{1}{x})^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9865ec07-4ed8-5e1e-a395-9077aca11780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere $u^{2} + v^{2} + w^{2} = 1$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128006} {:answer/id #uuid "c69a6d94-c00a-5d87-a560-a5f2f09e4ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128007} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfd4913-4ab5-5532-846b-462d3a33aa3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128008} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0934b8-9acf-5844-8c0e-424cfdf4b1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w^{2} = 2u$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128009} {:answer/id #uuid "73756ee3-5c09-5f7b-b85b-8b7272cf91bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w = u$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128010}] :answer-field/id #uuid "09a65432-6b6f-5c96-8820-a3d6b7b8d177" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f2fafa-94fa-537a-9de2-cde9b153270f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402683} {:answer/id #uuid "06b7dea6-5d67-5db6-9562-564a467e6927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402684} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdabe0b-331f-59b0-be6a-e0b333cf7d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder ${w}^{2}=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402685} {:answer/id #uuid "edca51bc-5f25-52b9-81ee-5e945aaea583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402686} {:answer/id #uuid "2daa5374-5e80-5f12-9e7f-d5612c496bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f2fafa-94fa-537a-9de2-cde9b153270f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402683} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec45c948-28d5-5ad6-a116-f2bf6a3a31b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab44c8de-700d-51cc-ab4a-3755f7926aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-39709" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}]}
